NAME
       lvs - list logical volumes, one line each

SYNOPSIS
       lvs [-a] [-o FIELDS] [-O SORT] [-S SELECTION] [--units U] [--noheadings]
           [--nosuffix] [--separator S] [--segments] [VG|LV...]

DESCRIPTION
       lvs prints a short table of logical volumes: name, volume group,
       attributes and size, plus columns used by snapshots, thin pools and
       mirrors. With VG or LV names it lists only those. It changes nothing,
       so run it as often as you like; it is the quickest way to check an LV
       you just made or resized. Run it as root.

       The Attr column is a row of letters. The first is the volume type
       (- for a plain LV, s snapshot, t thin pool...), the second w or r
       (writable or read-only), the third the allocation policy (i
       inherited), the fifth a when the LV is active, the sixth o when it is
       open (mounted or used as swap).

       Sizes are shown in a readable unit by default, with a < sign when the
       number is rounded down (<1.80g).

OPTIONS
       -o, --options FIELDS
              Show these columns (comma separated). With a leading + they are
              added to the default ones. Useful fields: lv_name, vg_name,
              lv_size, lv_attr, lv_path, lv_dm_path, devices, lv_uuid,
              seg_count, lv_active.

       -O, --sort FIELDS
              Sort by these fields.

       -S, --select EXPRESSION
              Show only rows that match, such as -S 'lv_size>1g'.

       -a, --all
              Include hidden internal volumes.

       --units U
              Show sizes in one unit: b, k, m, g, t (powers of 1024), or the
              capital letters for powers of 1000. h means readable (default).

       --noheadings
              Leave out the header line (for scripts).

       --nosuffix
              Leave the unit letter off sizes (use with --units).

       --separator S
              Separate the columns with S instead of aligning them.

       --segments
              One line per segment, showing where each part of the LV lives.

EXAMPLES
       Show all LVs:

           lvs

       Show the size of one LV in MiB, as a bare number, for a script:

           lvs --noheadings --units m --nosuffix -o lv_size vgdata/data

       See which disks hold each LV:

           lvs -o +devices

SEE ALSO
       pvs(8), vgs(8), lvdisplay(8), lvscan(8), lvm(8)
